1
00:00:00,210 --> 00:00:03,190
It is September 26, 1991,

2
00:00:04,090 --> 00:00:05,470
sunrise over the Arizona desert,

3
00:00:05,950 --> 00:00:07,450
35 miles north of Tucson.

4
00:00:09,010 --> 00:00:11,250
Eight people are about to be sealed inside this building,

5
00:00:11,590 --> 00:00:14,630
with 4,000 other species and no way to get food,

6
00:00:15,070 --> 00:00:17,250
water, or air from the outside for two years.

7
00:00:18,810 --> 00:00:21,190
200,000 cubic meters of sealed glass,

8
00:00:21,650 --> 00:00:26,150
6,500 windows, the tightest seal anyone had ever built around a piece of the living

9
00:00:26,150 --> 00:00:29,350
world. 500 people came to watch.

10
00:00:29,950 --> 00:00:31,810
The man closing the door is not a scientist.

11
00:00:32,510 --> 00:00:34,470
He is a Texas oil heir named Ed Bass,

12
00:00:34,910 --> 00:00:36,150
and this building is his money.

13
00:00:37,650 --> 00:00:38,870
He steps up to the airlock,

14
00:00:39,230 --> 00:00:41,590
he swings it shut, and it bounces back open.

15
00:00:43,830 --> 00:00:47,350
The reporter from the Arizona Daily Star writes it down exactly as it happens.

16
00:00:47,970 --> 00:00:49,430
The door popped back open,

17
00:00:49,650 --> 00:00:51,790
he writes, a fraction of a second later,

18
00:00:52,330 --> 00:00:54,130
as if one of the eight had second thoughts.

19
00:00:55,370 --> 00:00:58,270
It takes two tries to lock eight human beings inside.

20
00:00:58,890 --> 00:01:01,410
At 8:18 in the morning, the seal holds.

21
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:08,080
16 months later, the air inside this building will be missing 7 tons of oxygen,

22
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:11,500
and not one person on earth will be able to tell you where it went.

23
00:01:13,810 --> 00:01:17,150
It was not leaking out. The building was far too tight for that.

24
00:01:17,470 --> 00:01:18,730
It was going somewhere inside.

25
00:01:21,010 --> 00:01:22,090
You're watching mindwired.

26
00:01:23,390 --> 00:01:25,430
This is the story of Biosphere 2.

27
00:01:25,890 --> 00:01:27,690
What it cost, what it promised,

28
00:01:28,010 --> 00:01:30,230
what actually happened to the air inside it,

29
00:01:30,510 --> 00:01:33,750
and why the answer took two of the best geochemists alive to find.

30
00:01:35,070 --> 00:01:36,930
Not the engineers who built that seal.

31
00:01:37,770 --> 00:01:41,050
Not the chemists inside, testing the air every single day.

32
00:01:41,510 --> 00:01:45,210
And not the doctor, who would turn out to be the first person to notice something was

33
00:01:45,210 --> 00:01:49,250
wrong with him. And why, 35 years later,

34
00:01:49,570 --> 00:01:51,690
the thing everybody laughed at is still standing,

35
00:01:52,170 --> 00:01:56,090
still sealed, and still running experiments nobody else on earth can run.

36
00:01:57,570 --> 00:01:59,870
Chapter one, the ranch before the glass.

37
00:02:01,570 --> 00:02:03,530
To understand how a building like this gets built,

38
00:02:03,870 --> 00:02:05,710
you have to go back to a ranch in New Mexico,

39
00:02:06,090 --> 00:02:09,430
22 years earlier. In 1969,

40
00:02:10,310 --> 00:02:14,750
a man named John Polk Allen founded an intentional community 15 miles south of Santa

41
00:02:14,750 --> 00:02:16,790
Fe. He called it Synergia Ranch.

42
00:02:18,350 --> 00:02:19,570
Allen was not a hippie drifter.

43
00:02:19,990 --> 00:02:24,110
He had a degree in metallurgical and mining engineering from the Colorado School of Mines

44
00:02:24,110 --> 00:02:26,710
and an MBA from Harvard Business School.

45
00:02:27,170 --> 00:02:31,290
He also wrote poetry under the name Johnny Dolphin and ran a theater troop called the

46
00:02:31,290 --> 00:02:32,490
Theater of All Possibilities.

47
00:02:34,010 --> 00:02:37,970
In the early 1970s, the group founded something called the Institute of Ecotechnics.

48
00:02:38,410 --> 00:02:39,450
They built a research ship.

49
00:02:39,850 --> 00:02:41,390
They ran projects on four continents.

50
00:02:43,030 --> 00:02:44,190
And somewhere in that decade,

51
00:02:44,510 --> 00:02:46,870
at that ranch, John Allen met Ed Bass,

52
00:02:47,370 --> 00:02:50,850
heir to a Fort Worth oil fortune and one of the wealthiest men in Texas.

53
00:02:52,390 --> 00:02:54,610
In 1984, they launched the project together.

54
00:02:55,090 --> 00:02:56,710
Bass paid, Allen invented,

55
00:02:57,310 --> 00:03:01,050
and a designer and architect named Margret Augustine ran the company that would build

56
00:03:01,050 --> 00:03:06,110
it. Here is where the story splits and where I have to be careful with you because

57
00:03:06,110 --> 00:03:07,970
almost everyone in it is still alive.

58
00:03:09,490 --> 00:03:14,350
In 1991, a reporter for The Village Voice named Mark Cooper published a piece about the

59
00:03:14,350 --> 00:03:15,830
people behind Biosphere 2.

60
00:03:16,250 --> 00:03:17,570
He wrote that they were not,

61
00:03:17,990 --> 00:03:21,770
in his words, a group of high-tech researchers on the cutting edge of science.

62
00:03:23,350 --> 00:03:27,270
They were, he wrote, a clique of recycled theater performers that had evolved out of

63
00:03:27,270 --> 00:03:31,390
an authoritarian and decidedly non-scientific personality cult.

64
00:03:33,670 --> 00:03:37,650
That sentence did more damage to Biosphere 2 than any scientific result ever did.

65
00:03:38,290 --> 00:03:41,910
And it is an allegation by a named journalist in a named magazine.

66
00:03:42,390 --> 00:03:47,270
It is not a finding. Because here's the part that almost never gets quoted alongside

67
00:03:47,270 --> 00:03:52,370
it. The most credentialed early critic of the project was a botanist named

68
00:03:52,370 --> 00:03:55,750
Ghillean Prance, the director of the Royal Botanic Gardens at Kew.

69
00:03:56,330 --> 00:03:59,610
In 1983, he said their interest in science was not genuine,

70
00:03:59,650 --> 00:04:03,190
that they seemed guided by some sort of religious or philosophical system.

71
00:04:04,650 --> 00:04:06,170
Prance then designed their rainforest.

72
00:04:08,510 --> 00:04:10,810
And in 1991, asked about it again,

73
00:04:11,110 --> 00:04:13,190
he said this, they are visionaries.

74
00:04:13,750 --> 00:04:15,150
And maybe to fulfill their vision,

75
00:04:15,250 --> 00:04:16,670
they have become somewhat cult like.

76
00:04:17,430 --> 00:04:19,090
But they are not a cult, per se.

77
00:04:20,610 --> 00:04:22,550
The man who first used the word took it back.

78
00:04:23,150 --> 00:04:26,030
Both are on the record. And if you only ever hear one of them,

79
00:04:26,330 --> 00:04:27,470
you are being sold a verdict.

80
00:04:29,840 --> 00:04:33,120
In 1987, discover magazine called Biosphere 2,

81
00:04:33,260 --> 00:04:37,540
the most exciting scientific project undertaken in the United States since Kennedy launched

82
00:04:37,540 --> 00:04:40,400
us toward the moon. In 1992,

83
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:43,340
the journal Ecology called it New Age Drivel,

84
00:04:43,360 --> 00:04:47,480
masquerading as science. Same building,

85
00:04:48,100 --> 00:04:50,740
five years apart. Both of those are real quotes.

86
00:04:51,140 --> 00:04:52,620
And the truth is somewhere in the middle.

87
00:04:53,040 --> 00:04:56,100
And the only way to find it is to look at what the building actually did.

88
00:04:57,560 --> 00:04:59,720
Chapter 2, the number that made it science.

89
00:05:01,500 --> 00:05:06,400
3.14 acres under glass, 7,200,000 cubic feet of sealed air,

90
00:05:06,820 --> 00:05:09,760
6,500 windows. And underneath all of it,

91
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:14,780
a welded stainless steel liner weighing 500 tons so that nothing could seep up out of

92
00:05:14,780 --> 00:05:18,720
the ground. Inside, five wilderness biomes,

93
00:05:19,220 --> 00:05:24,500
a rainforest, an ocean, 700,000 gallons of it with a coral reef and

94
00:05:24,500 --> 00:05:26,880
a machine to make waves, mangrove wetlands,

95
00:05:27,520 --> 00:05:29,380
a savannah, a fog desert,

96
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:33,500
plus a farm and a place for the humans to live.

97
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:37,140
Roughly 4,000 species went in,

98
00:05:37,480 --> 00:05:41,900
most of them collected by the crew themselves on expeditions to jungles and reefs and

99
00:05:41,900 --> 00:05:44,960
deserts. Roy Walford, the crew doctor,

100
00:05:45,540 --> 00:05:46,660
later said the place became,

101
00:05:47,140 --> 00:05:50,320
in his words, our baby. Now,

102
00:05:50,760 --> 00:05:54,500
here's the number that decides whether any of this was science or theater and almost

103
00:05:54,500 --> 00:05:55,940
nobody tells you about it.

104
00:05:57,960 --> 00:06:00,260
A greenhouse leaks. Every building leaks.

105
00:06:00,860 --> 00:06:02,140
The question is how much.

106
00:06:03,280 --> 00:06:06,120
The engineer responsible for the seal was William Dempster.

107
00:06:07,740 --> 00:06:11,780
He pumped the building to a slight overpressure and watched how fast it sagged,

108
00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:15,360
and he spiked the air with three gases that react with nothing,

109
00:06:16,220 --> 00:06:17,840
sulfur, hexafluoride, helium,

110
00:06:17,980 --> 00:06:20,940
and krypton, and watched how fast they thinned.

111
00:06:21,820 --> 00:06:26,680
Both methods agreed. Biosphere 2 leaked less than 10% of its atmosphere per year.

112
00:06:28,760 --> 00:06:30,340
That number means nothing to you yet.

113
00:06:30,840 --> 00:06:32,840
So here is Dempster's own way of saying it.

114
00:06:33,920 --> 00:06:37,260
There were about 80 kilometers of cocked glazing joints in that building.

115
00:06:37,260 --> 00:06:39,940
About 16 kilometers of welded seam.

116
00:06:40,520 --> 00:06:43,480
Call it 50 miles of sealed glass and 10 miles of weld.

117
00:06:45,840 --> 00:06:47,080
And every flaw in all of it,

118
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:49,400
every pinhole, every bad seal,

119
00:06:49,860 --> 00:06:52,100
every imperfect weld added together,

120
00:06:52,360 --> 00:06:56,140
came to the equivalent of one single hole about 19 millimeters across,

121
00:06:58,740 --> 00:06:59,680
three-quarters of an inch,

122
00:07:00,100 --> 00:07:02,420
in a building the size of two and a half football fields.

123
00:07:03,800 --> 00:07:07,020
Hold on to that, because it is the reason there is a story at all.

124
00:07:09,280 --> 00:07:10,820
There was a second engineering problem,

125
00:07:11,260 --> 00:07:14,260
and the solution to it is the strangest looking thing on the property.

126
00:07:15,840 --> 00:07:18,300
Seal a building this tight and heat it with desert sun,

127
00:07:18,680 --> 00:07:19,900
and the air inside expands.

128
00:07:20,460 --> 00:07:23,500
It has nowhere to go, nothing to push against but glass.

129
00:07:24,840 --> 00:07:27,900
A wire service reporter put it plainly on the day they sealed it.

130
00:07:28,540 --> 00:07:32,100
Otherwise, he wrote, the heat and pressure would burst the glass walls.

131
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:35,500
So they built lungs, two of them,

132
00:07:35,880 --> 00:07:39,140
enormous domed chambers connected to the main structure by tunnels,

133
00:07:39,620 --> 00:07:44,520
each one containing a vast flexible diaphragm that rose and fell as the air inside expanded

134
00:07:44,520 --> 00:07:47,560
and contracted. The building breathed,

135
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:50,520
not as a metaphor. Mechanically,

136
00:07:50,880 --> 00:07:55,890
every day, with the sun. And it worked so well

137
00:07:55,890 --> 00:07:59,650
that the pressure difference between the inside and the outside of Biosphere 2 stayed

138
00:07:59,650 --> 00:08:03,730
under eight Pascals, about one-thousandth of a pound per square inch.

139
00:08:05,070 --> 00:08:08,290
There is one more detail from the construction that I think about a lot.

140
00:08:09,870 --> 00:08:11,370
They built a fog desert inside.

141
00:08:11,950 --> 00:08:13,070
It did not stay a desert.

142
00:08:13,910 --> 00:08:17,990
Water condensed on the underside of the steel and glass above it and dripped back down,

143
00:08:18,350 --> 00:08:21,330
and over time that section drifted toward Chaparral,

144
00:08:21,670 --> 00:08:22,950
a wetter kind of scrubland.

145
00:08:24,370 --> 00:08:26,170
The building rained on its own desert.

146
00:08:26,910 --> 00:08:30,890
Nobody designed that. It is the first hint of the thing this whole story is about,

147
00:08:30,990 --> 00:08:33,370
which is that a sealed world does what it wants,

148
00:08:33,770 --> 00:08:37,460
not what you drew. Chapter 3.

149
00:08:37,980 --> 00:08:40,620
What They Ate. Eight people.

150
00:08:41,220 --> 00:08:45,880
Four women, four men, a split chosen deliberately after looking at how Antarctic bases

151
00:08:45,880 --> 00:08:47,540
and submarine crews hold together.

152
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,480
Roy Walford, 67. The Doctor.

153
00:08:52,260 --> 00:08:55,060
Abigail Alling, 31. Marine Biologist,

154
00:08:55,540 --> 00:08:57,280
first through the door. Mark Nelson,

155
00:08:57,840 --> 00:09:00,010
44. Wastewater and wetlands.

156
00:09:00,590 --> 00:09:05,050
Linda Leigh, 39. Botanist. Taber MacCallum,

157
00:09:05,210 --> 00:09:07,850
27. Running the Analytical Chemistry Lab.

158
00:09:08,690 --> 00:09:11,110
Jane Poynter, 29. Farm Manager.

159
00:09:12,090 --> 00:09:16,210
Sally Silverstone, 36. Co-captain and Tropical Agriculture.

160
00:09:17,010 --> 00:09:19,590
Mark Van Thillo, 30. Technical Systems.

161
00:09:21,290 --> 00:09:24,710
Their job was to grow their own food inside two thousand square meters of farm.

162
00:09:25,230 --> 00:09:30,070
On paper it worked. Over the full two years they averaged about 2,200 calories a day

163
00:09:30,070 --> 00:09:35,310
each. On paper. In the first six

164
00:09:35,310 --> 00:09:39,710
months the crops failed and the real number was 1,780 calories a day.

165
00:09:41,110 --> 00:09:42,570
Broad mites got into the crops.

166
00:09:43,210 --> 00:09:44,810
Root knot nematodes got into the soil.

167
00:09:45,330 --> 00:09:50,010
In the two years they chose landed on back-to-back El Niño years so the sky over Arizona

168
00:09:50,010 --> 00:09:52,350
was cloudier than the building had been designed for.

169
00:09:53,750 --> 00:09:59,130
Less light. Less food. Mark Nelson went in at 147 pounds and bottomed

170
00:09:59,130 --> 00:10:03,950
out at 117. Taber MacCallum lost 46 pounds in six months.

171
00:10:05,430 --> 00:10:09,210
Across the full two years the crew lost about 17% of their body weight.

172
00:10:10,810 --> 00:10:12,010
Nelson wrote about it afterwards.

173
00:10:12,890 --> 00:10:14,870
We experienced hunger throughout the two years,

174
00:10:15,110 --> 00:10:17,490
he wrote, and plates were always licked clean.

175
00:10:18,230 --> 00:10:22,330
And then this. I and many others ate our roasted peanuts whole,

176
00:10:22,770 --> 00:10:26,050
shell and all. We would eat anything to fill the stomach void.

177
00:10:28,380 --> 00:10:31,100
On the day they came out a reporter asked him about it.

178
00:10:31,640 --> 00:10:35,320
Nelson said the hardest thing was that hunger was a constant nagging presence.

179
00:10:35,800 --> 00:10:39,360
He said it was hard even to watch a movie because he kept focusing on what the actors

180
00:10:39,360 --> 00:10:44,470
were eating. Jane Poynter said it took her four months to make a pizza.

181
00:10:44,870 --> 00:10:48,870
She had to grow the wheat and thresh it and feed and milk the goats for the cheese.

182
00:10:50,350 --> 00:10:54,370
They ate so many sweet potatoes that their palms turned orange with beta carotene.

183
00:10:55,250 --> 00:10:58,730
Coffee was rationed to about one cup per person every few weeks.

184
00:11:00,250 --> 00:11:04,150
And now the uncomfortable part and I want to lay it out fairly because the man at the

185
00:11:04,150 --> 00:11:06,050
center of it is dead and cannot answer.

186
00:11:07,590 --> 00:11:09,190
Roy Walford was the crew's only physician.

187
00:11:09,890 --> 00:11:14,210
He was also at that time the most prominent researcher in the world on the idea that

188
00:11:14,210 --> 00:11:16,950
eating substantially less food extends human lifespan.

189
00:11:17,610 --> 00:11:19,710
He had founded the calorie restriction society.

190
00:11:19,710 --> 00:11:22,210
He had been on that diet himself for years.

191
00:11:23,750 --> 00:11:27,810
The crew's daily food allotments were planned using Walford's own diet planning computer

192
00:11:27,810 --> 00:11:31,710
program. That is stated plainly in a paper written by two of the crew.

193
00:11:33,110 --> 00:11:37,710
And when the farm failed and the calories fell he published the crew as research subjects

194
00:11:37,710 --> 00:11:42,910
twice in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences and in a gerontology

195
00:11:42,910 --> 00:11:47,210
journal. The mission's biggest failure became his data set.

196
00:11:49,680 --> 00:11:51,080
Walford knew exactly how that looked.

197
00:11:51,500 --> 00:11:54,100
He said so himself, unprompted.

198
00:11:55,180 --> 00:11:57,600
I think if there had been any other nutritionist or physician,

199
00:11:57,900 --> 00:12:00,000
he said, they would have freaked out and said,

200
00:12:00,380 --> 00:12:04,160
we're starving. But I knew we were actually on a program of health enhancement.

201
00:12:06,550 --> 00:12:08,590
You can read that two ways and both are honest.

202
00:12:09,210 --> 00:12:13,390
The experiment's worst failure and the doctor's life's work pointed the same direction

203
00:12:13,390 --> 00:12:16,510
and he was the only person inside qualified to make the call.

204
00:12:17,750 --> 00:12:22,170
For what it is worth his own follow-up paper concluded that all eight of them came out

205
00:12:22,170 --> 00:12:24,810
in excellent health. That is also on the record.

206
00:12:27,030 --> 00:12:29,650
Chapter four, the thing nobody invited.

207
00:12:31,270 --> 00:12:32,510
While the humans were getting thinner,

208
00:12:32,930 --> 00:12:34,510
the sealed world was doing something worse.

209
00:12:36,090 --> 00:12:38,590
Of 25 small vertebrate species they put inside,

210
00:12:39,490 --> 00:12:44,430
19 went extinct. And every single pollinating insect species died.

211
00:12:47,020 --> 00:12:50,280
Think about what that means in a building where the humans have to grow their own food.

212
00:12:50,780 --> 00:12:54,740
The flowering plants inside Biosphere 2 could no longer reproduce on their own.

213
00:12:55,160 --> 00:12:57,240
In a sealed world, that is a countdown.

214
00:12:58,700 --> 00:13:01,560
What thrived instead was a species nobody had invited.

215
00:13:03,080 --> 00:13:05,580
When they surveyed the site in 1990 and 91,

216
00:13:05,980 --> 00:13:09,820
before closure, no ant species was dominant and one particular ant,

217
00:13:10,180 --> 00:13:12,820
the Longhorn crazy ant, was not present at all,

218
00:13:13,200 --> 00:13:15,380
not one. It got in as a stowaway.

219
00:13:16,860 --> 00:13:22,160
By 1996, more than 99.9% of every ant that came to a bait station

220
00:13:22,160 --> 00:13:24,220
inside Biosphere 2 was that one species.

221
00:13:26,560 --> 00:13:31,640
They did it by farming. The ants tended enormous populations of sap sucking insects on

222
00:13:31,640 --> 00:13:34,440
the plants and lived off the honeydew those insects produced.

223
00:13:35,060 --> 00:13:38,620
A closed loop inside a closed world that nobody planned.

224
00:13:40,060 --> 00:13:41,820
Alongside them, cockroaches,

225
00:13:42,240 --> 00:13:46,300
which had been deliberately introduced to break down dead material and which also ran

226
00:13:46,300 --> 00:13:51,240
away with the place. The scientists who later wrote the most damning assessment of the

227
00:13:51,240 --> 00:13:55,560
whole project described what was left inside as crazy ants running everywhere,

228
00:13:56,040 --> 00:13:58,420
together with scattered cockroaches and katydids.

229
00:14:00,670 --> 00:14:03,130
Chapter 5. The air starts going.

230
00:14:04,570 --> 00:14:07,150
Taber MacCallum ran the analytical chemistry lab inside.

231
00:14:07,730 --> 00:14:09,390
Every day he measured the air.

232
00:14:11,090 --> 00:14:12,990
Normal air is 20.9% oxygen.

233
00:14:13,590 --> 00:14:17,950
That is what they sealed in with on the 26th of September 1991.

234
00:14:19,510 --> 00:14:21,770
Five months later it was 18%.

235
00:14:23,370 --> 00:14:25,890
That is not a drift. That is a cliff.

236
00:14:26,670 --> 00:14:30,610
And it almost never gets told because everyone jumps to the famous number at the end.

237
00:14:31,970 --> 00:14:34,570
Then the fall slowed down and kept going.

238
00:14:35,170 --> 00:14:37,270
Month after month, for over a year,

239
00:14:37,750 --> 00:14:41,770
roughly 140 parts per million of oxygen disappearing every single day.

240
00:14:43,030 --> 00:14:46,130
Now remember the seal. 50 miles of glass joint,

241
00:14:46,470 --> 00:14:50,630
10 miles of weld, one 19 millimeter holes worth of leak in the whole building.

242
00:14:52,090 --> 00:14:53,330
The oxygen was not escaping.

243
00:14:54,050 --> 00:14:58,770
It could not escape. It was disappearing inside a sealed box and the same measurement

244
00:14:58,770 --> 00:15:01,710
that proved it was disappearing also proved it had nowhere to go.

245
00:15:04,130 --> 00:15:08,130
And there is a cruel piece of physics in here that I need you to understand because it

246
00:15:08,130 --> 00:15:10,530
is why this got dangerous rather than just strange.

247
00:15:12,150 --> 00:15:13,170
When you climb a mountain,

248
00:15:13,610 --> 00:15:15,310
the oxygen percentage stays the same.

249
00:15:15,830 --> 00:15:16,910
What drops is the pressure.

250
00:15:17,490 --> 00:15:19,110
Your body knows how to respond to that.

251
00:15:19,550 --> 00:15:21,550
Over weeks it makes more red blood cells.

252
00:15:21,950 --> 00:15:25,570
You acclimatize. Inside Biosphere 2,

253
00:15:25,710 --> 00:15:26,650
the pressure never dropped.

254
00:15:27,030 --> 00:15:29,910
The lungs saw to that. Only the oxygen fraction fell.

255
00:15:31,330 --> 00:15:33,110
So their bodies never got the signal.

256
00:15:33,630 --> 00:15:38,190
They were living at the equivalent of 13 to 15,000 feet and they could not adapt to

257
00:15:38,190 --> 00:15:40,490
it because as far as their physiology was concerned,

258
00:15:40,590 --> 00:15:42,970
they were still at 3,800 feet in Arizona.

259
00:15:45,490 --> 00:15:47,010
They started developing sleep apnea,

260
00:15:47,910 --> 00:15:51,210
waking in the night gasping because their blood chemistry had changed.

261
00:15:52,750 --> 00:15:57,430
So they ran oxygen lines from the analytical laboratory to the bedrooms and slept breathing

262
00:15:57,430 --> 00:16:01,110
supplemented air inside the sealed world that was supposed to make its own.

263
00:16:03,540 --> 00:16:05,340
Jane Poynter described what those months were like.

264
00:16:05,840 --> 00:16:07,720
We were dragging ourselves around the biosphere,

265
00:16:07,980 --> 00:16:10,520
she said, and everybody outside thought we were dying.

266
00:16:10,980 --> 00:16:14,500
The media was making it sound like we were dying and I had to call up my mother every

267
00:16:14,500 --> 00:16:16,480
other day saying, no, mom,

268
00:16:16,840 --> 00:16:18,220
it's fine. We're not dead.

269
00:16:20,620 --> 00:16:21,820
And she gave it the name that stuck.

270
00:16:22,340 --> 00:16:24,300
It was like playing atomic hide and seek,

271
00:16:24,660 --> 00:16:29,240
she said. We had lost seven tons of oxygen and we had no clue where it was.

272
00:16:31,560 --> 00:16:34,300
Chapter 6. The seal was never a seal.

273
00:16:35,880 --> 00:16:37,160
While the oxygen was vanishing,

274
00:16:37,640 --> 00:16:41,840
the project's credibility was doing the same thing and for entirely separate reasons.

275
00:16:43,260 --> 00:16:44,800
About 12 days into the mission,

276
00:16:45,300 --> 00:16:49,280
Jane Poynter was feeding rice into a threshing machine on the farm and it took off the

277
00:16:49,280 --> 00:16:51,100
tip of the middle finger of her left hand.

278
00:16:52,720 --> 00:16:54,100
Walford reattached it inside.

279
00:16:54,900 --> 00:16:57,380
It failed. So on the 11th of October,

280
00:16:57,800 --> 00:17:01,360
1991, 16 days into a two year sealed mission,

281
00:17:01,840 --> 00:17:05,080
they opened the airlock and Jane Poynter walked out to a hospital.

282
00:17:07,430 --> 00:17:08,610
She came back the same day,

283
00:17:09,130 --> 00:17:11,770
out for a few hours. She lost the fingertip anyway.

284
00:17:13,330 --> 00:17:15,130
On its own, that would have been a footnote.

285
00:17:15,890 --> 00:17:18,110
A medical emergency, handled,

286
00:17:18,650 --> 00:17:22,010
disclosed. Except they did not disclose it.

287
00:17:22,510 --> 00:17:23,730
Not for almost three months.

288
00:17:24,190 --> 00:17:27,910
The public did not learn that the seal had been broken until the 4th of January,

289
00:17:28,310 --> 00:17:32,990
1992. And when she came back through that airlock,

290
00:17:33,450 --> 00:17:34,970
she carried a duffel bag in with her.

291
00:17:35,730 --> 00:17:39,850
What was inside it has never been settled and I'm going to give you all three versions

292
00:17:39,850 --> 00:17:42,050
because all three of those people are alive.

293
00:17:43,430 --> 00:17:46,570
The company's own inventory said the bag held plastic bags,

294
00:17:46,970 --> 00:17:49,530
two reference books, maps showing where plants were,

295
00:17:49,950 --> 00:17:53,030
color film, hydrochloric acid and spare computer parts.

296
00:17:54,570 --> 00:17:56,810
Poynter's own account is that it held nothing of substance.

297
00:17:57,650 --> 00:17:59,350
Some circuit boards, she said,

298
00:17:59,630 --> 00:18:01,350
and a planting plan for the rainforest.

299
00:18:02,990 --> 00:18:04,130
And then there is the allegation.

300
00:18:04,790 --> 00:18:06,570
A filmmaker named Lewis Hawthorne,

301
00:18:06,830 --> 00:18:08,890
who had spent weeks on the site during preparation,

302
00:18:09,450 --> 00:18:13,810
told the Associated Press that his sources among the staff described two bags containing

303
00:18:13,810 --> 00:18:19,070
steel fittings, hydrochloric acid and a handful of lead wire airlock tamper indicators.

304
00:18:20,550 --> 00:18:23,790
The tamper indicators are the seals fitted to the outside of the doors.

305
00:18:24,310 --> 00:18:27,130
They are the physical proof that nobody has gone in or out.

306
00:18:29,380 --> 00:18:31,960
The company spokesman called that patently ridiculous.

307
00:18:32,560 --> 00:18:35,140
I have no way to settle it and neither does anyone else.

308
00:18:35,700 --> 00:18:40,100
All three accounts include the hydrochloric acid and they differ on everything that matters.

309
00:18:41,300 --> 00:18:43,360
But the bag is not what broke them.

310
00:18:43,640 --> 00:18:47,550
This is. On the 19th of December,

311
00:18:47,990 --> 00:18:53,090
1991, the project acknowledged that it had installed a carbon dioxide scrubber inside

312
00:18:53,090 --> 00:18:55,390
Biosphere 2 and had not told anybody.

313
00:18:56,810 --> 00:19:00,610
A machine that pulled carbon dioxide out of the air by blowing it through a falling curtain

314
00:19:00,610 --> 00:19:04,830
of sodium hydroxide solution in a world that was supposed to be balancing its own atmosphere

315
00:19:04,830 --> 00:19:07,730
with plants. On the same day,

316
00:19:07,990 --> 00:19:12,790
they also disclosed that 600,000 cubic feet of outside air had been pumped in to replace

317
00:19:12,790 --> 00:19:17,850
air that had leaked out. That is the moment the press turned and

318
00:19:17,850 --> 00:19:22,530
it never turned back and the project does have an answer in the peer-reviewed record.

319
00:19:23,990 --> 00:19:25,890
The scrubber was not a last-minute panic.

320
00:19:26,370 --> 00:19:31,590
It was designed in from the start in anticipation of the seasonal swing between photosynthesis

321
00:19:31,590 --> 00:19:35,110
in summer and respiration in winter and crucially,

322
00:19:35,130 --> 00:19:36,890
it was designed to be reversible.

323
00:19:38,410 --> 00:19:42,790
There was a furnace whose job was to bake the captured carbon back out again in the summer

324
00:19:42,790 --> 00:19:44,830
and turn it to the air, closing the loop.

325
00:19:46,270 --> 00:19:47,450
Its heating elements failed.

326
00:19:48,230 --> 00:19:49,510
The loop was never closed.

327
00:19:50,190 --> 00:19:51,870
Across the entire two years,

328
00:19:52,210 --> 00:19:54,930
that furnace never once did the job it existed to do.

329
00:19:57,140 --> 00:20:01,640
So, which is it? A cover-up or a broken machine that a suspicious press found at the

330
00:20:01,640 --> 00:20:06,140
worst possible moment? Here's the fact that settles what it meant,

331
00:20:06,440 --> 00:20:11,920
if not what it was. That scrubber removed about 98 kilomoles of carbon dioxide around

332
00:20:11,920 --> 00:20:16,920
4.3 tons. Set against the amount of oxygen that had gone missing.

333
00:20:17,600 --> 00:20:18,900
That accounted for almost nothing.

334
00:20:19,640 --> 00:20:22,040
Something like one and a half percent of the atmospheric carbon.

335
00:20:23,620 --> 00:20:24,700
Whatever the scrubber was,

336
00:20:24,880 --> 00:20:26,180
it was not where the oxygen went.

337
00:20:26,620 --> 00:20:31,320
The mystery was still completely open and the crew had 13 months of it left to live through.

338
00:20:33,870 --> 00:20:36,150
Chapter 7. The doctor couldn't add.

339
00:20:37,530 --> 00:20:40,690
By January 1993, 16 months in,

340
00:20:40,950 --> 00:20:45,430
the oxygen inside Biosphere 2 had fallen to about 14.2%.

341
00:20:46,740 --> 00:20:48,500
Roy Walford was the doctor.

342
00:20:49,180 --> 00:20:52,940
67 years old, the oldest person inside by more than 20 years.

343
00:20:53,460 --> 00:20:55,780
His job was to monitor everybody else's health.

344
00:20:57,200 --> 00:20:58,880
He was also, it turned out,

345
00:20:59,200 --> 00:21:01,940
the most susceptible to the thin air of anyone in the building.

346
00:21:03,500 --> 00:21:05,180
Jane Poynter tells what happened next,

347
00:21:05,480 --> 00:21:09,320
and it is the moment this whole story turns from a science experiment into something

348
00:21:09,320 --> 00:21:12,620
else. And one day, she said,

349
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:14,420
he couldn't add up a line of figures.

350
00:21:16,910 --> 00:21:18,910
And it was time for us to put oxygen in.

351
00:21:21,190 --> 00:21:24,610
The man measuring everyone else's decline was the instrument that finally registered

352
00:21:24,610 --> 00:21:26,890
it. He disqualified himself from duty.

353
00:21:27,650 --> 00:21:28,730
He could not do arithmetic.

354
00:21:30,310 --> 00:21:32,510
On a winter night in January 1993,

355
00:21:33,290 --> 00:21:34,630
they broke the seal on purpose.

356
00:21:36,210 --> 00:21:40,030
Between day 475 and day 494 of the closure,

357
00:21:40,030 --> 00:21:43,630
pure oxygen was pumped into Biosphere 2 from the outside world.

358
00:21:45,470 --> 00:21:47,730
19 days of it. Not a single dramatic moment,

359
00:21:48,030 --> 00:21:49,930
whatever you have read. A slow,

360
00:21:50,310 --> 00:21:53,250
deliberate, engineered rescue of a building's atmosphere.

361
00:21:54,890 --> 00:21:58,450
Mark Nelson wrote about the moment they opened the door between their world and the fresh

362
00:21:58,450 --> 00:22:03,690
air on the other side. We left a world with an oxygen level around 14%,

363
00:22:03,690 --> 00:22:07,190
he wrote, equivalent to being on a 15,000 foot mountain.

364
00:22:07,750 --> 00:22:11,830
In fact, we were at a 3,900 foot elevation in southern Arizona.

365
00:22:13,530 --> 00:22:15,870
Oxygen had been slowly disappearing for 16 months.

366
00:22:16,490 --> 00:22:17,530
No one knew where it had gone.

367
00:22:18,050 --> 00:22:19,370
We were slowly climbing a mountain,

368
00:22:19,690 --> 00:22:23,210
but going nowhere. And then,

369
00:22:23,270 --> 00:22:25,430
when the air came in. In minutes,

370
00:22:25,870 --> 00:22:29,330
we felt decades younger. For the first time in many months,

371
00:22:29,710 --> 00:22:31,150
I heard the sound of running feet.

372
00:22:33,860 --> 00:22:39,080
Chapter 8. In the Walls. So where did seven tons of oxygen go?

373
00:22:39,500 --> 00:22:42,680
Inside a building that leaked the equivalent of one three quarter inch hole.

374
00:22:44,080 --> 00:22:46,400
The obvious answer had been on the table from the beginning,

375
00:22:46,840 --> 00:22:47,940
and it had been ruled out.

376
00:22:48,540 --> 00:22:51,540
The reason it was ruled out is the most interesting thing in this story.

377
00:22:53,060 --> 00:22:54,340
Here is the obvious answer.

378
00:22:54,940 --> 00:22:57,180
Soil is alive. It is full of microbes,

379
00:22:57,680 --> 00:23:01,140
and microbes breathe. They take in oxygen and organic matter,

380
00:23:01,340 --> 00:23:04,320
and they give off carbon dioxide exactly the way you do.

381
00:23:05,500 --> 00:23:09,700
And the designers of Biosphere 2 had deliberately packed their soils with organic matter,

382
00:23:09,980 --> 00:23:11,580
because they wanted the farm to be fertile.

383
00:23:12,240 --> 00:23:13,140
They wanted things to grow.

384
00:23:14,620 --> 00:23:16,720
So the soil microbes had an enormous banquet,

385
00:23:17,180 --> 00:23:19,300
and they ate it, and they breathed while they did.

386
00:23:19,860 --> 00:23:21,560
Respiration outran photosynthesis.

387
00:23:22,240 --> 00:23:23,860
The building's soil was eating its air.

388
00:23:25,400 --> 00:23:27,760
Simple, obvious, and provably wrong,

389
00:23:27,980 --> 00:23:32,750
because of one number. Respiration is a one-for-one trade.

390
00:23:33,450 --> 00:23:37,490
Every molecule of oxygen that goes in comes back out as a molecule of carbon dioxide.

391
00:23:38,170 --> 00:23:40,330
If the soil had eaten seven tons of oxygen,

392
00:23:40,670 --> 00:23:43,890
the carbon dioxide inside that sealed building should have gone through the roof.

393
00:23:45,350 --> 00:23:49,620
It didn't. Dempster wrote it down plainly.

394
00:23:50,460 --> 00:23:53,720
The obvious explanation of loss to respiration was not evident,

395
00:23:53,880 --> 00:23:57,720
he wrote, because the implied increase of carbon dioxide did not occur.

396
00:23:58,320 --> 00:24:01,860
And furthermore, the atmospheric volume of Biosphere 2 was shrinking.

397
00:24:03,200 --> 00:24:06,080
So the mystery was never really that oxygen was vanishing.

398
00:24:06,080 --> 00:24:09,840
The mystery was that carbon dioxide was not appearing to match it.

399
00:24:10,340 --> 00:24:11,700
Two gases should have moved together.

400
00:24:12,220 --> 00:24:13,300
One of them didn't show up.

401
00:24:15,500 --> 00:24:19,380
Which meant something inside that building was taking the carbon dioxide out of the air

402
00:24:19,380 --> 00:24:21,240
as fast as the soil was making it.

403
00:24:21,500 --> 00:24:23,040
Silently. Without a machine.

404
00:24:23,700 --> 00:24:24,560
Without anybody noticing.

405
00:24:26,160 --> 00:24:27,320
They brought in outside help.

406
00:24:28,200 --> 00:24:32,020
Wallace Broecker, one of the most important geochemists of the 20th century.

407
00:24:32,500 --> 00:24:35,700
The man who put the phrase global warming into the scientific literature.

408
00:24:36,240 --> 00:24:38,580
And a young researcher named Jeffrey Severinghaus.

409
00:24:40,020 --> 00:24:41,120
And they did something clever.

410
00:24:41,920 --> 00:24:43,880
They went looking for the carbon by its fingerprint.

411
00:24:45,340 --> 00:24:46,440
Carbon comes in different weights.

412
00:24:47,180 --> 00:24:50,400
Carbon 12 and carbon 13. And living things are slightly fussy.

413
00:24:51,040 --> 00:24:52,980
When a plant or a microbe processes carbon,

414
00:24:53,200 --> 00:24:54,440
it prefers the lighter one,

415
00:24:54,680 --> 00:24:56,480
which leaves a measurable signature behind.

416
00:24:57,780 --> 00:25:01,540
So carbon that has been through something alive is isotopically different from carbon

417
00:25:01,540 --> 00:25:03,220
that hasn't. It is a tag.

418
00:25:03,220 --> 00:25:05,180
And it does not wash off.

419
00:25:06,420 --> 00:25:08,360
They measured the isotopes in the atmosphere.

420
00:25:08,900 --> 00:25:11,300
In the biomass. In the scrubbers output.

421
00:25:12,040 --> 00:25:12,940
And then in the concrete.

422
00:25:15,210 --> 00:25:18,770
Biosphere 2 had 15,800 square meters of exposed,

423
00:25:19,370 --> 00:25:20,870
unsealed concrete inside it.

424
00:25:21,030 --> 00:25:22,750
And concrete, when it is young,

425
00:25:23,010 --> 00:25:25,730
is not inert. It cures. It reacts.

426
00:25:27,510 --> 00:25:31,650
Calcium oxide in the concrete pulls carbon dioxide straight out of the air and locks

427
00:25:31,650 --> 00:25:33,010
it up as calcium carbonate.

428
00:25:33,430 --> 00:25:35,370
As limestone. Permanently.

429
00:25:36,890 --> 00:25:39,270
And when they measured the carbon locked inside that concrete,

430
00:25:39,690 --> 00:25:42,570
it carried the isotopic signature of something that had been alive.

431
00:25:45,180 --> 00:25:49,200
The inner surfaces held roughly 10 times the calcium carbonate of the outer ones.

432
00:25:49,760 --> 00:25:53,520
Somewhere between 600 and 750 kilomoles of carbon dioxide.

433
00:25:54,040 --> 00:25:55,960
On the order of 25 tons of it.

434
00:25:56,440 --> 00:26:00,250
Sitting in the walls. That is the answer.

435
00:26:00,890 --> 00:26:02,430
The soil microbes ate the oxygen.

436
00:26:02,750 --> 00:26:04,910
Exactly as anyone would have guessed on day one.

437
00:26:04,910 --> 00:26:08,330
And then the building's own concrete quietly ate the evidence.

438
00:26:10,950 --> 00:26:12,090
Two reactions in sequence.

439
00:26:12,430 --> 00:26:13,690
In a place nobody was looking.

440
00:26:14,230 --> 00:26:19,150
One famous. One so boring it happens on every construction site on earth and nobody thinks

441
00:26:19,150 --> 00:26:24,330
about it once. Eight people nearly suffocated because their house was still drying.

442
00:26:27,120 --> 00:26:29,800
Chapter 9. The only building that could have seen it.

443
00:26:30,940 --> 00:26:32,800
And now I can tell you why the seal mattered.

444
00:26:33,100 --> 00:26:36,080
And why I asked you to hold on to that 19 millimeter hole.

445
00:26:37,380 --> 00:26:41,140
The oxygen was falling by about 140 parts per million a day.

446
00:26:41,960 --> 00:26:46,360
That is a small signal. Dempster ran the simulations afterwards to find out how leaky

447
00:26:46,360 --> 00:26:49,420
a building would have to be before that signal disappeared into the noise.

448
00:26:50,860 --> 00:26:52,900
The answer was a leak rate of 1% a day.

449
00:26:53,360 --> 00:26:57,380
Not a year. A day. At that rate,

450
00:26:57,860 --> 00:27:01,260
fresh air seeps in fast enough to top the oxygen back up as it goes.

451
00:27:01,700 --> 00:27:03,580
An ordinary greenhouse would never have noticed.

452
00:27:05,180 --> 00:27:07,500
Biosphere 2 leaked less than 10% in a year.

453
00:27:07,520 --> 00:27:09,660
It was, by a wide margin,

454
00:27:10,040 --> 00:27:11,800
tight enough to detect its own suffocation.

455
00:27:14,360 --> 00:27:16,120
And here is the detail that finishes the argument.

456
00:27:16,740 --> 00:27:19,960
And it is a sad one. After 1996,

457
00:27:20,760 --> 00:27:24,320
when the project changed hands and the building was reconfigured for a different kind

458
00:27:24,320 --> 00:27:26,960
of research, they stopped maintaining that seal.

459
00:27:27,520 --> 00:27:30,820
The leak rate degraded to somewhere between 1 and 2% a day.

460
00:27:31,320 --> 00:27:36,680
About 50 times worse. Which means the experiment that made Biosphere 2 famous.

461
00:27:36,680 --> 00:27:39,600
The one thing it discovered that nobody else could have discovered,

462
00:27:39,860 --> 00:27:42,060
could never have been detected in the building it became.

463
00:27:44,600 --> 00:27:46,740
It got exactly one chance to find that out.

464
00:27:47,060 --> 00:27:49,600
And the reason it found it is the thing everyone was laughing at.

465
00:27:49,900 --> 00:27:51,680
The obsessive, expensive,

466
00:27:52,720 --> 00:27:53,860
ridiculous tightness of the seal.

467
00:27:55,720 --> 00:28:01,180
Chapter 10 The Door Opens From Outside They came out on the 26th

468
00:28:01,180 --> 00:28:04,940
of September, 1993. Two years and about 20 minutes.

469
00:28:06,580 --> 00:28:08,040
Abigail Alling was first out.

470
00:28:08,500 --> 00:28:11,900
A reporter watching noted that she appeared to be trying to catch her breath as she stepped

471
00:28:11,900 --> 00:28:14,560
to the microphone. What she managed to say was,

472
00:28:14,900 --> 00:28:16,720
it's really a very different atmosphere.

473
00:28:18,990 --> 00:28:21,930
They had also, by then, stopped speaking to each other.

474
00:28:22,430 --> 00:28:24,430
The eight had split into two groups of four.

475
00:28:25,710 --> 00:28:29,990
I want to be careful, because the crew member who wrote the peer-reviewed paper on that

476
00:28:29,990 --> 00:28:32,750
split deliberately declined to name who was on which side,

477
00:28:33,090 --> 00:28:35,190
and I am not going to do what he chose not to.

478
00:28:36,350 --> 00:28:38,450
What is on the record is what the fight was about,

479
00:28:38,870 --> 00:28:40,110
and it is not what you would expect.

480
00:28:40,790 --> 00:28:44,130
It was not cabin fever. It was an argument that came from outside.

481
00:28:45,730 --> 00:28:48,670
The project's owners were at war over the direction of the whole enterprise,

482
00:28:49,090 --> 00:28:50,790
and that war propagated inside.

483
00:28:51,550 --> 00:28:53,590
One group wanted to hold the closure absolutely.

484
00:28:54,430 --> 00:28:56,470
The other wanted to loosen it and import food.

485
00:28:58,050 --> 00:29:01,110
Nelson compared it to the oldest argument on any Antarctic expedition.

486
00:29:01,950 --> 00:29:03,310
The sailors against the scientists.

487
00:29:04,610 --> 00:29:07,270
Six months later, that war came for the building.

488
00:29:09,380 --> 00:29:12,200
A second crew of seven was sealed in on the 6th of March,

489
00:29:12,540 --> 00:29:15,140
1994. On the 1st of April,

490
00:29:15,620 --> 00:29:19,540
Federal Marshals arrived with a restraining order and removed the entire on-site management

491
00:29:19,540 --> 00:29:22,460
team. The new crew told what was happening,

492
00:29:22,800 --> 00:29:24,580
initially thought it was an April Fool's joke.

493
00:29:26,100 --> 00:29:27,480
Ed Bass put a banker in charge,

494
00:29:28,040 --> 00:29:30,220
a man who ran a Beverly Hills investment firm,

495
00:29:30,460 --> 00:29:33,400
and who would become considerably more famous two decades later,

496
00:29:33,740 --> 00:29:37,100
named Steve Bannon. Three days after that,

497
00:29:37,380 --> 00:29:41,000
at three in the morning, two members of the original eight came back to the building

498
00:29:41,000 --> 00:29:42,600
they had lived inside for two years,

499
00:29:42,960 --> 00:29:48,160
and opened it. Abigail Alling and Mark Van Thillo opened one double airlock

500
00:29:48,160 --> 00:29:51,700
and three emergency exits and left them open for about 15 minutes.

501
00:29:52,240 --> 00:29:55,320
Roughly a tenth of the sealed atmosphere exchanged with the Arizona air.

502
00:29:55,860 --> 00:29:57,360
Five panes of glass were broken.

503
00:29:59,670 --> 00:30:01,350
They had been suspended three days earlier.

504
00:30:02,290 --> 00:30:03,670
Alling's account, on the record,

505
00:30:03,950 --> 00:30:07,910
is this. I considered the biosphere to be in an emergency state.

506
00:30:08,450 --> 00:30:09,730
In no way was it sabotage.

507
00:30:10,530 --> 00:30:11,530
It was my responsibility.

508
00:30:13,170 --> 00:30:16,870
The company called it sabotage and was still calling it that two years later.

509
00:30:17,690 --> 00:30:19,750
Both of those are positions held by living people,

510
00:30:20,010 --> 00:30:21,850
and I am not going to pick between them for you.

511
00:30:23,030 --> 00:30:24,390
They were charged with burglary,

512
00:30:25,010 --> 00:30:26,550
trespassing, and criminal damage,

513
00:30:26,970 --> 00:30:29,350
and released on $25,000 bail each,

514
00:30:29,770 --> 00:30:31,650
and I have to be straight with you about something,

515
00:30:31,770 --> 00:30:36,150
because it matters. I could not find out what happened to those charges.

516
00:30:36,850 --> 00:30:38,150
Two separate research passes,

517
00:30:38,610 --> 00:30:41,970
and the disposition of that criminal case is not in the public record I can reach.

518
00:30:42,350 --> 00:30:44,790
No verdict, no plea, no dismissal.

519
00:30:45,390 --> 00:30:47,070
So I am not going to imply one.

520
00:30:49,280 --> 00:30:51,680
What I do know is that in June 1996,

521
00:30:52,100 --> 00:30:56,320
a jury in Florence, Arizona found in their favor in a separate civil case,

522
00:30:56,600 --> 00:31:01,880
over being fired. The Associated Press reported the award is nearly $520,000

523
00:31:01,880 --> 00:31:06,200
in lost wages. The second mission was shut down early,

524
00:31:06,680 --> 00:31:08,940
on the 6th of September, 1994.

525
00:31:10,040 --> 00:31:12,520
Nobody was ever sealed inside Biosphere 2 again.

526
00:31:15,070 --> 00:31:19,790
Chapter 11 The Verdict In November 1996,

527
00:31:20,370 --> 00:31:24,990
two ecologists, Joel Cohen of Rockefeller University and David Tillman of Minnesota,

528
00:31:25,490 --> 00:31:29,170
published the assessment in science that has defined Biosphere 2 ever since.

529
00:31:30,690 --> 00:31:33,230
It is not gentle. Despite the resources,

530
00:31:33,650 --> 00:31:37,970
they wrote, it proved impossible to create a materially closed system that could support

531
00:31:37,970 --> 00:31:39,590
eight humans with adequate food,

532
00:31:40,070 --> 00:31:41,530
water, and air for two years.

533
00:31:43,030 --> 00:31:44,310
And the line that stings most,

534
00:31:45,090 --> 00:31:49,050
almost unlimited energy and technology were available to support Biosphere 2 from the

535
00:31:49,050 --> 00:31:54,110
outside. Their conclusion is short enough to read to you in full,

536
00:31:54,550 --> 00:31:59,070
and it is, I think, one of the better paragraphs written about our situation on this

537
00:31:59,070 --> 00:32:04,170
planet. At present, there is no demonstrated alternative to maintaining the

538
00:32:04,170 --> 00:32:08,670
viability of Earth. No one yet knows how to engineer systems that provide humans with

539
00:32:08,670 --> 00:32:12,070
the life-supporting services that natural ecosystems produce for free.

540
00:32:13,470 --> 00:32:15,050
Despite its mysteries and hazards,

541
00:32:15,510 --> 00:32:18,110
Earth remains the only known home that can sustain life.

542
00:32:20,690 --> 00:32:22,970
That is the prosecution, and it is fair,

543
00:32:23,410 --> 00:32:24,690
and it is quoted constantly.

544
00:32:25,250 --> 00:32:27,690
The paragraph immediately before it almost never is.

545
00:32:28,670 --> 00:32:30,590
There may be a partial analogy,

546
00:32:30,770 --> 00:32:34,570
they wrote, between the initial problems of Biosphere 2 and the early,

547
00:32:34,910 --> 00:32:37,370
well-publicized flaws of the Hubble Space Telescope.

548
00:32:38,150 --> 00:32:39,410
Now that Hubble has been improved,

549
00:32:39,850 --> 00:32:43,650
it is a major instrument with the potential for observations never possible before.

550
00:32:45,190 --> 00:32:50,170
Similarly, they wrote, research in a retooled Biosphere 2 may well contribute exciting

551
00:32:50,170 --> 00:32:53,650
insights into the task of maintaining the viability of Biosphere 1,

552
00:32:54,110 --> 00:32:59,170
the Earth. The harshest critics Biosphere 2 ever had left the

553
00:32:59,170 --> 00:33:02,110
door open, and then history walked through it.

554
00:33:04,130 --> 00:33:09,450
Chapter 12, What it Became Columbia University ran it from 1996

555
00:33:09,450 --> 00:33:14,510
to 2003, and used that 700,000 -gallon ocean to demonstrate what

556
00:33:14,510 --> 00:33:16,930
rising carbon dioxide does to a coral reef.

557
00:33:18,430 --> 00:33:22,450
In 2007, the property was sold for $50 million to a developer,

558
00:33:22,450 --> 00:33:27,330
with 1,500 homes and a resort hotel already approved for the land around it.

559
00:33:27,810 --> 00:33:29,370
Demolition was a real possibility.

560
00:33:31,050 --> 00:33:34,110
The University of Arizona took over operations that same year.

561
00:33:34,650 --> 00:33:36,750
In 2011, they were given the building outright,

562
00:33:37,090 --> 00:33:39,630
along with $20 million from Ed Bass to run it.

563
00:33:39,910 --> 00:33:42,150
He gave another $30 million in 2017.

564
00:33:43,630 --> 00:33:45,210
It is open to the public today,

565
00:33:45,570 --> 00:33:47,910
about 100,000 people a year walk through it.

566
00:33:48,770 --> 00:33:53,030
And inside, they built something called the Landscape Evolution Observatory.

567
00:33:53,730 --> 00:33:55,010
Three artificial hill slopes,

568
00:33:55,410 --> 00:34:00,270
each 30 meters long, each filled a meter deep with 500 tons of crushed basalt,

569
00:34:00,930 --> 00:34:02,630
sterile ground of volcanic rock,

570
00:34:03,130 --> 00:34:07,050
dead. More than 1,800 sensors in each one,

571
00:34:07,330 --> 00:34:09,950
watching what happens when you add water to bear rock and weight.

572
00:34:11,310 --> 00:34:14,090
What happened is that nobody put anything alive on those slopes,

573
00:34:14,410 --> 00:34:15,450
and life showed up anyway.

574
00:34:18,230 --> 00:34:20,350
Cyanobacteria colonized the sterile basalt.

575
00:34:21,410 --> 00:34:23,790
Photosynthesizing, nitrogen-fixing crusts,

576
00:34:23,810 --> 00:34:25,030
forming on ground-up rock,

577
00:34:25,310 --> 00:34:27,190
exactly the way they did on the early Earth.

578
00:34:28,110 --> 00:34:30,170
Mosses now blanket those slopes seasonally.

579
00:34:31,550 --> 00:34:34,410
The principal investigator's summary of the result was three words.

580
00:34:34,850 --> 00:34:38,260
Life finds a way. In April,

581
00:34:38,380 --> 00:34:43,040
2026, they were funded to take the next step and build miniature versions of those hill

582
00:34:43,040 --> 00:34:45,720
slopes running Martian atmosphere and Martian soil chemistry.

583
00:34:46,760 --> 00:34:51,160
And in February, 2026, for the first time in more than 30 years,

584
00:34:51,660 --> 00:34:54,120
they put corals back into the Biosphere 2 ocean,

585
00:34:55,160 --> 00:34:58,840
108 living fragments from the Florida Keys and the Gulf of Mexico.

586
00:35:00,400 --> 00:35:05,560
Columbia used that same water 30 years ago to prove that carbon dioxide kills reefs.

587
00:35:06,280 --> 00:35:08,780
Today, it is being used to find the ones that survive.

588
00:35:11,110 --> 00:35:12,810
I want to tell you what I actually think,

589
00:35:13,010 --> 00:35:15,090
because I have spent a long time with this one.

590
00:35:16,550 --> 00:35:18,330
Biosphere 2 is remembered as a joke.

591
00:35:19,050 --> 00:35:20,710
There is a Paulie Shore movie about it,

592
00:35:20,830 --> 00:35:24,770
with a 4% score on Rotten Tomatoes and a 1 out of 100 on Metacritic,

593
00:35:25,030 --> 00:35:28,070
which ties it for the worst-reviewed film on that entire website.

594
00:35:28,650 --> 00:35:33,070
They shot it at a water reclamation plant in Van Nuys because the real Biosphere 2 turned

595
00:35:33,070 --> 00:35:36,150
them down. Here is the thing about the joke.

596
00:35:36,710 --> 00:35:39,130
Biosphere 2 failed at the thing it was sold as.

597
00:35:39,510 --> 00:35:42,690
It could not keep eight people fed and breathing for two years without help.

598
00:35:42,690 --> 00:35:45,330
That is true, and there is no way around it.

599
00:35:46,730 --> 00:35:49,290
But a failure that teaches you something real is not a stunt.

600
00:35:49,690 --> 00:35:54,730
It is an experiment, and the discovery it produced that a sealed world's oxygen can vanish

601
00:35:54,730 --> 00:35:59,790
into its own walls while its carbon dioxide reading stays flat and reassuring is cited

602
00:35:59,790 --> 00:36:02,330
in the reference list of the very paper that condemned it.

603
00:36:03,550 --> 00:36:04,850
Nobody would have predicted that.

604
00:36:05,470 --> 00:36:09,370
Nobody did predict it. And nobody could have found it in a building that was even slightly

605
00:36:09,370 --> 00:36:12,550
less well sealed. Bad engineering.

606
00:36:13,330 --> 00:36:15,110
Worse governance. Genuine science.

607
00:36:15,830 --> 00:36:19,670
And a building that has outlived every single reason people had for laughing at it.

608
00:36:22,050 --> 00:36:25,070
Jane Poynter said something about coming out that I cannot stop thinking about.

609
00:36:25,550 --> 00:36:28,970
For two years, she said, I'd been seeing people through the glass.

610
00:36:30,490 --> 00:36:34,670
When visitors came, there was a window with a telephone on the outside and a telephone

611
00:36:34,670 --> 00:36:38,030
on the inside. And you would put your hand flat against the pane,

612
00:36:38,230 --> 00:36:40,470
and they would put theirs against it on the other side,

613
00:36:40,590 --> 00:36:45,930
and that was hello. Eight people spent two years learning in

614
00:36:45,930 --> 00:36:49,570
the most literal way anybody ever has that air is not a given.

615
00:36:50,090 --> 00:36:55,130
That it is made constantly by things too small to see and can be taken away

616
00:36:55,130 --> 00:36:56,990
by a wall that has not finished drying.

617
00:36:58,290 --> 00:37:00,950
They built a second earth to find out how the first one works.

618
00:37:01,590 --> 00:37:04,050
It didn't work. That is how they found out.

619
00:37:06,450 --> 00:37:09,830
If the idea of microbes quietly rewriting an atmosphere got to you,

620
00:37:09,830 --> 00:37:12,610
there is a version of that story on a scale you will not believe,

621
00:37:12,950 --> 00:37:18,010
and it involves the sun. I made a video about whether the sun-killing microbe in

622
00:37:18,010 --> 00:37:19,950
Project Hail Mary could actually exist.

623
00:37:20,610 --> 00:37:22,870
Real astrobiology, real extremophiles,

624
00:37:23,350 --> 00:37:24,810
and an answer that surprised me.

625
00:37:25,170 --> 00:37:28,710
It is on screen now. And if you want more of these,

626
00:37:29,010 --> 00:37:31,610
the ones built out of the actual papers rather than the headlines,

627
00:37:32,170 --> 00:37:34,470
subscribe to mindwired. Thanks for watching.

628
00:37:45,550 --> 00:37:47,710
Right now, it costs you nothing,

629
00:37:48,050 --> 00:37:51,370
and it's the entire reason mindwired keeps making videos like this.

